That's on purpose.

00:14:54.260 --> 00:14:56.780
And the reason for that is
two of the contenders, two

00:14:56.780 --> 00:14:58.850
of the competitors that
we looked at, simply

00:14:58.850 --> 00:15:02.820
didn't even have this language
to score in the first place.

00:15:02.820 --> 00:15:06.080
So that's kind of another reason
why we found Google AutoML

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:07.900
Translation to be so powerful--

00:15:07.900 --> 00:15:10.880
the rich coverage of languages
that some other competitors

00:15:10.880 --> 00:15:13.450
simply don't have at all.

00:15:13.450 --> 00:15:15.940
So let's take a look
at another case.

00:15:15.940 --> 00:15:21.090
So this is also IT domain for
another Fortune 1000 client.

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:25.140
And so what we
often do is we look

00:15:25.140 --> 00:15:28.810
at auto scoring, which is very
cheap and efficient to do,

00:15:28.810 --> 00:15:31.860
but we also measure
human evaluation.

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:35.190
So we'll take some
sentences as a sample,

00:15:35.190 --> 00:15:37.410
and we'll have a human
being look at them

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:42.750
and adjudicate them based
on a scale of 1 to, 5 with 1

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:44.940
as the worst, 5 as the best.

00:15:44.940 --> 00:15:47.010
And the idea here
is we want to see

00:15:47.010 --> 00:15:50.640
to what degree this lines
up with the patterns

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:53.100
that we're seeing
from auto scoring.

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:55.930
And here, again, you
see a similar situation.

00:15:55.930 --> 00:15:58.200
So what we're looking
for is usability

00:15:58.200 --> 00:15:59.880
on a scale from 1 to 5.

00:15:59.880 --> 00:16:03.390
Usability means to what
degree are the sentences

00:16:03.390 --> 00:16:05.770
understandable and actionable?

00:16:05.770 --> 00:16:08.400
And there are two
use cases for this.

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:11.880
One is you might want
to do just raw machine

00:16:11.880 --> 00:16:14.260
translation, as Olga was
mentioning with Open Table.

00:16:14.260 --> 00:16:16.740
So it's going to come out
with raw machine translation.

00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:17.280
That's it.

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:19.360
It's not going to be
post edited at all.

00:16:19.360 --> 00:16:21.600
So we need to figure
out how good this

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:23.700
is because no one's
going to touch it.

00:16:23.700 --> 00:16:26.490
The other scenario
is post-editing--

00:16:26.490 --> 00:16:29.710
so we're going to post edit
this up to a human level.

00:16:29.710 --> 00:16:32.700
And again, the better
the initial quality

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:35.040
is, the less we have to post
edit, the more productive

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:37.990
we are, and the more
discounts we can give.

00:16:37.990 --> 00:16:40.470
So what we have
here is a table that

00:16:40.470 --> 00:16:43.590
shows a hundred sentences
that were evaluated

00:16:43.590 --> 00:16:46.830
in these various competitors,
on a scale from 1 to 5--

00:16:46.830 --> 00:16:48.420
it's an aggregate score.

00:16:48.420 --> 00:16:51.390
And what you'll see across the
board for all of the languages

00:16:51.390 --> 00:16:54.914
is that Google
AutoML came in first.

00:16:54.914 --> 00:16:55.830
OLGA BEREGOVAYA: Now--

00:16:55.830 --> 00:16:59.770
ALEX YANISHEVSKY: Sorry--
so in this case, here,

00:16:59.770 --> 00:17:02.530
number one means that it
finished in first place,

00:17:02.530 --> 00:17:04.500
meaning it got the most 5s.

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:06.480
So sorry about that.

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:11.250
It was on a scale from 1 to 5,
but these are basically ranked.

00:17:11.250 --> 00:17:13.050
Google AutoML
Translation is the best,

00:17:13.050 --> 00:17:16.740
and number six here is
actually the worst performer.

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:19.119
Sorry if that was confusing.

00:17:19.119 --> 00:17:22.140
So the things that I
want to mention here

00:17:22.140 --> 00:17:26.099
is that you see that Google
AutoML Translation came in, was

00:17:26.099 --> 00:17:28.590
the best, in all
of the languages.

00:17:28.590 --> 00:17:31.860
And you see languages
here that span

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:34.860
romance languages, like
Spanish and French,

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:37.050
which are relatively
easy grammatically;

00:17:37.050 --> 00:17:40.140
to kind of mid-tier languages,
more complicated grammatically,

00:17:40.140 --> 00:17:42.090
like German and
Russian; and finally,

00:17:42.090 --> 00:17:45.540
Asian languages like Korean
and simplified Chinese, which

00:17:45.540 --> 00:17:47.250
are particularly
difficult and where

00:17:47.250 --> 00:17:50.400
we've seen the greatest
improvements on neural.

00:17:50.400 --> 00:17:52.410
And again, the
other thing here is

00:17:52.410 --> 00:17:57.270
that Google Cloud Translation,
of the six languages,

00:17:57.270 --> 00:18:00.960
it came in number two twice
and number three three times.

00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:04.560
So again, we kind of see this
picture of Google Cloud as very

00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:07.360
good to start out with,
but when we train it,

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:10.710
Google AutoML Translation
clearly comes out on top.

00:18:10.710 --> 00:18:13.710
And this actually
dovetails very well, again,

00:18:13.710 --> 00:18:17.320
with post-editing distance
for these six languages.

00:18:17.320 --> 00:18:19.470
So here, the gray bar--

00:18:19.470 --> 00:18:23.190
again, we're looking for lower
is better because less edits

00:18:23.190 --> 00:18:24.180
that you have to do.

00:18:24.180 --> 00:18:26.700
So the gray bar, the one
that's lowest for everything,

00:18:26.700 --> 00:18:29.190
is again, Google
AutoML Translation.

00:18:29.190 --> 00:18:31.620
And you could see, when
you look at second place,

00:18:31.620 --> 00:18:34.530
often second place
is green, which

00:18:34.530 --> 00:18:37.680
is Google Cloud Translation,
and the other ones

00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:39.199
are far, far worse.

00:18:39.199 --> 00:18:40.740
And you could see
that the difference

00:18:40.740 --> 00:18:44.610
between the gray and the green
is already fairly significant.

00:18:44.610 --> 00:18:46.470
So again, we're
seeing this picture

00:18:46.470 --> 00:18:51.690
of very, very good quality when
we train AutoML Translation.

00:18:51.690 --> 00:18:55.110
And as Sudheera said, we can
basically get these models back

00:18:55.110 --> 00:18:58.052
in three, four hours and start
benchmarking and rolling them

00:18:58.052 --> 00:18:59.385
out into production immediately.

00:18:59.385 --> 00:19:00.350
ALEX YANISHEVSKY:
And maybe I could

00:19:00.350 --> 00:19:02.266
say a couple of words
about the edit distance.

00:19:02.266 --> 00:19:04.770
As I said earlier, if in
the case of OpenTable,

00:19:04.770 --> 00:19:07.920
we don't know if we're
going to be post-editing

00:19:07.920 --> 00:19:10.350
or not going to be
post-editing-- in case

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:13.230
of Blackboard, for instance,
we do know for a fact

00:19:13.230 --> 00:19:15.320
that we are going
to be post-editing,

00:19:15.320 --> 00:19:19.650
and edit distance is our
core decision making factor

00:19:19.650 --> 00:19:22.920
when we know how we're going to
be negotiating with our client

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:24.810
and how we're going
to be negotiating

00:19:24.810 --> 00:19:26.160
with our translators.

00:19:26.160 --> 00:19:29.400
So basically knowing what
we get for edit distance

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:31.620
is the way that we're
basically going to be--

00:19:31.620 --> 00:19:34.020
that's the way we're going to
be structuring our business

00:19:34.020 --> 00:19:36.580
conversations on both
sides of the equation.

00:19:36.580 --> 00:19:39.180
This is why it's the
key metric for us.

00:19:39.180 --> 00:19:42.280
ALEX YANISHEVSKY:
Thank you very much.

00:19:42.280 --> 00:19:45.208
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:48.150 --> 00:19:50.010
SUDHEERA VANGURI:
Thanks, Alex and Olga.

00:19:50.010 --> 00:19:55.760
Moving on, I'd like to take
you into a product walk-through

00:19:55.760 --> 00:19:59.020
of Cloud Natural Language
API and then dive

00:19:59.020 --> 00:20:01.660
deeper into AutoML Natural
Language, one of our newest

00:20:01.660 --> 00:20:03.550
product offerings.

00:20:03.550 --> 00:20:07.180
Cloud national Language
API that's currently in GA

00:20:07.180 --> 00:20:10.660
currently supports four
different features--

00:20:10.660 --> 00:20:14.680
entity extraction-- assume you'd
like to extract some key words

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:21.670
or phrases from a text
passage; sentiment analysis,

00:20:21.670 --> 00:20:27.490
how positively or negatively
biased a certain article is,

00:20:27.490 --> 00:20:31.900
what the sentiment of, say, a
user review; analyze syntax--

00:20:31.900 --> 00:20:35.920
say you'd like to get into the
linguistic details or the parts

00:20:35.920 --> 00:20:38.830
of speech of a
certain text message--

00:20:38.830 --> 00:20:41.930
what's a noun, what's an
adjective, so on and so forth;

00:20:41.930 --> 00:20:44.740
and finally,
classifying content.

00:20:44.740 --> 00:20:47.650
Our current content
classification API

00:20:47.650 --> 00:20:50.650
available within Cloud
Natural Language API

00:20:50.650 --> 00:20:54.340
supports 700 different
categories and subcategories,

00:20:54.340 --> 00:20:57.420
and this is based on
a predefined taxonomy.

00:20:57.420 --> 00:21:01.600
This taxonomy, this
content classification API

00:21:01.600 --> 00:21:05.470
is trained on massive
volumes of Google data,

00:21:05.470 --> 00:21:09.370
and it's highly suitable
for various categories.

00:21:09.370 --> 00:21:11.500
But what we have
had our customers

00:21:11.500 --> 00:21:15.780
ask us is to customize these
categories to their own use

00:21:15.780 --> 00:21:16.480
cases.

00:21:16.480 --> 00:21:20.350
Imagine an oil and gas customer,
or a health care customer,

00:21:20.350 --> 00:21:22.180
or certainly again
technical data,

00:21:22.180 --> 00:21:26.470
where niche classifications
or labels or categories

00:21:26.470 --> 00:21:30.550
are very custom to an
enterprise use case.

00:21:30.550 --> 00:21:35.830
User reviews, customer feedback
may need to be classified to,

00:21:35.830 --> 00:21:41.320
let's say, the there is a
flight survey experience that

00:21:41.320 --> 00:21:46.090
needs to be categorized into
ambience and service or food,

00:21:46.090 --> 00:21:46.910
so on and so forth.

00:21:46.910 --> 00:21:49.570
So these categories may be
very relevant and specific

00:21:49.570 --> 00:21:53.050
to a certain enterprise use
cases, a certain domain,

00:21:53.050 --> 00:21:54.970
and there may be
use cases where you

00:21:54.970 --> 00:22:00.160
can squeeze more accuracy out of
the content classification API.

00:22:00.160 --> 00:22:05.410
So that's one of the primary
factors for us diving deeper

00:22:05.410 --> 00:22:07.240
into AutoML Natural Language.

00:22:07.240 --> 00:22:08.830
And the first
feature that we have

00:22:08.830 --> 00:22:11.470
enabled as part of
AutoML Natural Language

00:22:11.470 --> 00:22:14.000
is Custom Content
Classification.

00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:16.270
Now how does this work?

00:22:16.270 --> 00:22:19.450
Again, similar to AutoML
Translation, customers

00:22:19.450 --> 00:22:24.420
can come in to AutoML mail with
some amount of training data.

00:22:24.420 --> 00:22:30.420
We require at least
10 examples per label

00:22:30.420 --> 00:22:33.040
as a minimum
requirement for running

00:22:33.040 --> 00:22:36.550
the model, and about hundred
examples for each label.

00:22:36.550 --> 00:22:38.890
That's very little amount
of training data that

00:22:38.890 --> 00:22:42.370
needs to be provided to train an
AutoML Natural Language Content

00:22:42.370 --> 00:22:44.050
Classification Model.

00:22:44.050 --> 00:22:46.240
Now once this
classifier is trained--

00:22:46.240 --> 00:22:49.900
and again, this is going to only
take about four to five hours

00:22:49.900 --> 00:22:55.070
of training on some amount
of training data, and boom,

00:22:55.070 --> 00:23:00.650
you get your custom classifier
that works specifically

00:23:00.650 --> 00:23:01.830
on your data.

00:23:01.830 --> 00:23:04.040
Your data is not
shared with Google

00:23:04.040 --> 00:23:09.470
or with any other
customer model or data.

00:23:09.470 --> 00:23:13.550
And the model can start
to scale as needed.

00:23:13.550 --> 00:23:15.740
Assume your model
gets very popular,

00:23:15.740 --> 00:23:18.180
if you start seeing
huge amount of requests,

00:23:18.180 --> 00:23:21.620
the model you can scale to
adopt to your customer demands.

00:23:21.620 --> 00:23:24.710
And if you have further
amount of training data,

00:23:24.710 --> 00:23:27.380
you can always retrain your
model, and then the custom

00:23:27.380 --> 00:23:30.050
classifier scales to
adapt to your needs

00:23:30.050 --> 00:23:34.030
your own labels and categories.

00:23:34.030 --> 00:23:35.840
So how could you use AutoML NL?

00:23:35.840 --> 00:23:37.680
Here's three examples.

00:23:37.680 --> 00:23:40.660
You could predict the tags
of a Stack Overflow question.

00:23:40.660 --> 00:23:46.320
Say-- this is a very technical
example that the Custom Content

00:23:46.320 --> 00:23:51.320
Classifier or regular classifier
APIs may not be able to catch.

00:23:51.320 --> 00:23:55.550
You could predict which
news house a certain article

00:23:55.550 --> 00:23:57.980
is from.

00:23:57.980 --> 00:24:00.670
And health care--
assume doctor's notes,

00:24:00.670 --> 00:24:03.530
a patient's notes
need to be analyzed.

00:24:03.530 --> 00:24:07.510
Again, 90% of enterprise
data is unstructured,

00:24:07.510 --> 00:24:12.420
so given an input of
health care specific terms,

00:24:12.420 --> 00:24:15.600
you could provide the
model some training data

00:24:15.600 --> 00:24:17.730
to tags symptoms
to certain diseases

00:24:17.730 --> 00:24:21.030
and the AutoML Classifier
can start predicting

00:24:21.030 --> 00:24:23.340
based on this custom use case.

00:24:23.340 --> 00:24:25.530
Behind the scenes,
what do we do?

00:24:25.530 --> 00:24:30.030
Again, if you note, throughout
this experience we did not

00:24:30.030 --> 00:24:31.530
talk about
hyperparametric tuning,

00:24:31.530 --> 00:24:34.950
we did not talk about anything
other than just training

00:24:34.950 --> 00:24:40.070
your data, clicking on Train,
looking at evaluation metrics.

00:24:40.070 --> 00:24:42.180
If the model's good, great.

00:24:42.180 --> 00:24:44.610
Then if the model isn't, you
provide more training data

00:24:44.610 --> 00:24:46.590
or tweak the model to
your specific use case

00:24:46.590 --> 00:24:48.450
and then you run predictions.

00:24:48.450 --> 00:24:51.600
Behind the scenes, we mimic
make a data scientist--

00:24:51.600 --> 00:24:54.330
what a data scientist would
do to generate the best

00:24:54.330 --> 00:24:56.340
model for your use case.

00:24:56.340 --> 00:25:01.260
We kick off multiple pipelines,
multiple Google models,

00:25:01.260 --> 00:25:05.820
and we surface the best model
for that specific customer

00:25:05.820 --> 00:25:06.940
data set.

00:25:06.940 --> 00:25:11.310
It's abstracted
from the customer

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:15.470
which parameters need to be
tuned, which model to be used.

00:25:15.470 --> 00:25:17.820
We do all the heavy lifting
behind the scenes, ,

00:25:17.820 --> 00:25:21.540
and you see the best model that
gives you the highest precision

00:25:21.540 --> 00:25:24.600
recall for your use case.

00:25:24.600 --> 00:25:27.540
We've seen a lot of customer
excitement for AutoML.

00:25:27.540 --> 00:25:30.120
We've been in alpha
for a while, and we've

00:25:30.120 --> 00:25:32.400
had a lot of
customers, one of whom

00:25:32.400 --> 00:25:37.140
is going to talk
in a brief while--

00:25:37.140 --> 00:25:38.190
Qualtrics.

00:25:38.190 --> 00:25:43.020
But we've seen
Meredith specifically

00:25:43.020 --> 00:25:47.087
on Natural Language,
Hearst, Singapore Airlines.

00:25:47.087 --> 00:25:49.170
There's a lot of customers
within the news segment

00:25:49.170 --> 00:25:52.200
and various other domains that
have found significant use

00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:54.750
cases within AutoML
Natural Language,

00:25:54.750 --> 00:25:57.155
and within Translate
as well-- we

00:25:57.155 --> 00:26:00.900
see Welocalize and plenty
of other customers.

00:26:00.900 --> 00:26:04.440
With that, I would love to
go into some of the details

00:26:04.440 --> 00:26:06.990
that Qualtrics can talk about.

00:26:06.990 --> 00:26:09.600
They've used AutoML
Natural Language

00:26:09.600 --> 00:26:12.750
for optimizing their
survey responses,

00:26:12.750 --> 00:26:15.900
and I'd love for Milind to
explain how they've done that.

00:26:18.874 --> 00:26:20.290
MILIND KOPIKARE:
Thanks, Sudheera.

00:26:20.290 --> 00:26:21.650
So hi, everyone.

00:26:21.650 --> 00:26:22.690
My name is Milind.

00:26:22.690 --> 00:26:25.780
I head product for
Qualtrics Surveys--

00:26:25.780 --> 00:26:28.480
our surveys, flagship product.

00:26:28.480 --> 00:26:32.800
And what I'll do is I'll
talk about the problem

00:26:32.800 --> 00:26:36.520
that the survey research
industry is facing,

00:26:36.520 --> 00:26:38.390
we talk about the
solution, and then

00:26:38.390 --> 00:26:41.980
we look at under the hood, how
we implemented that solution.

00:26:41.980 --> 00:26:45.820
We'll also show you
some cool demos.

00:26:45.820 --> 00:26:52.740
So with that, what's the
problem you're trying to solve?

00:26:52.740 --> 00:26:57.900
So at Qualtrics, every year,
we send out more than 200

00:26:57.900 --> 00:26:59.820
million-plus surveys.

00:26:59.820 --> 00:27:02.430
We have billions of
survey responses.

00:27:02.430 --> 00:27:06.412
And the problem is that--

00:27:06.412 --> 00:27:08.620
and this is just across the
survey research industry.

00:27:08.620 --> 00:27:10.890
In fact, you would see
better numbers here.

00:27:10.890 --> 00:27:16.410
But 85% or more of surveys
are just not completed.

00:27:16.410 --> 00:27:18.540
And I'm sure you can
all relate to that.

00:27:18.540 --> 00:27:20.930
You know, who loves
taking surveys?

00:27:20.930 --> 00:27:23.430
And the reason why
that's the case--

00:27:23.430 --> 00:27:25.410
more often than
not, the questions

00:27:25.410 --> 00:27:28.380
that people ask in surveys
are just plain wrong.

00:27:28.380 --> 00:27:32.880
They are not relevant questions,
they are not worded correctly,

00:27:32.880 --> 00:27:35.820
and often that means that
it results in invalid data.

00:27:35.820 --> 00:27:37.740
First of all, you'd
be lucky if people

00:27:37.740 --> 00:27:39.540
would complete the
survey, and even if they

00:27:39.540 --> 00:27:42.090
do, the famous saying, right?

00:27:42.090 --> 00:27:43.700
"Garbage in, garbage out."

00:27:43.700 --> 00:27:46.140
So if you ask a
wrong question, you

00:27:46.140 --> 00:27:48.930
are going to get a wrong answer.

00:27:48.930 --> 00:27:51.070
And that's a huge problem.

00:27:51.070 --> 00:27:54.090
It's resulting in invalid
data would mean wrong business

00:27:54.090 --> 00:27:56.760
decisions, which is
costing the survey research

00:27:56.760 --> 00:27:59.760
industry as much as $4
billion in losses every year.

00:27:59.760 --> 00:28:02.250
More importantly, wrong
business decisions

00:28:02.250 --> 00:28:04.920
means, to quote a phrase,
it's "sinking the ship"

00:28:04.920 --> 00:28:07.280
for a lot of businesses.

00:28:07.280 --> 00:28:11.570
Now, what's the
solution for this?

00:28:11.570 --> 00:28:14.570
Well, the solution
is pretty simple.

00:28:14.570 --> 00:28:16.400
Just create better surveys.

00:28:16.400 --> 00:28:18.620
Make sure that you ask
the right questions.

00:28:18.620 --> 00:28:20.960
If you are designing
a survey, make sure

00:28:20.960 --> 00:28:24.787
that the questions you
ask are very relevant.

00:28:24.787 --> 00:28:26.870
At Qualtrics, we call it
"predictive questioning,"

00:28:26.870 --> 00:28:29.510
and I'll show you
a demo of that.

00:28:29.510 --> 00:28:31.700
And if you are a
survey taker, let's

00:28:31.700 --> 00:28:35.720
make sure that you are having
an engaging experience.

00:28:35.720 --> 00:28:38.275
I think Sudheera mentioned
about post-flight surveys.

00:28:38.275 --> 00:28:40.400
Let's make sure that if
you're getting off a flight

00:28:40.400 --> 00:28:42.950
and you get a survey, and
if you say that, look,

00:28:42.950 --> 00:28:46.940
I am upset, that the
flight was messy,

00:28:46.940 --> 00:28:49.610
let's not ask a question
like, on a scale of 1 to 5,

00:28:49.610 --> 00:28:50.580
how happy are you?

00:28:50.580 --> 00:28:52.040
You clearly are upset.

00:28:52.040 --> 00:28:54.830
You'd better ask a more
relevant question to that.

00:28:54.830 --> 00:28:59.410
So a quick show of hands.

00:28:59.410 --> 00:29:02.620
How many of you, let's
say in the last one year,

00:29:02.620 --> 00:29:07.149
had a bad time taking a flight?

00:29:07.149 --> 00:29:08.190
So pretty much all of us.

00:29:08.190 --> 00:29:11.190
We all have the stories of
having a poor flight experience

00:29:11.190 --> 00:29:13.146
some time or the other.

00:29:13.146 --> 00:29:14.770
The thing is-- you
probably don't know,

00:29:14.770 --> 00:29:17.015
but a lot of flight
companies-- a lot of them are

00:29:17.015 --> 00:29:18.280
our customers--

00:29:18.280 --> 00:29:20.060
they do take it
seriously, and they do

00:29:20.060 --> 00:29:21.310
want to do something about it.

00:29:21.310 --> 00:29:23.170
They have armies of
people who are working

00:29:23.170 --> 00:29:24.878
on customer service,
working on their end

00:29:24.878 --> 00:29:26.880
to fix these problems.

00:29:26.880 --> 00:29:28.100
But the problem is--

00:29:28.100 --> 00:29:29.600
they are doing all
the right things,

00:29:29.600 --> 00:29:31.558
they're trying to get
feedback, but they're not

00:29:31.558 --> 00:29:32.990
asking the right questions.

00:29:32.990 --> 00:29:35.140
So while they have armies
of people on standby

00:29:35.140 --> 00:29:38.350
to fix these issues,
they're not able to find out

00:29:38.350 --> 00:29:41.920
what is the issue that you,
as you walk out of the flight,

00:29:41.920 --> 00:29:45.300
had with the flight
you just took.

00:29:45.300 --> 00:29:48.140
So what I'll do is I'll show
you a quick demo, where we

00:29:48.140 --> 00:29:51.230
took a regular flight survey.

00:29:51.230 --> 00:29:52.930
It will be very familiar.

00:29:52.930 --> 00:29:53.900
You all must have--

00:29:53.900 --> 00:29:56.780
from your favorite flight, and
you got off, you got a survey,

00:29:56.780 --> 00:29:58.202
and you answered questions.

00:29:58.202 --> 00:29:59.660
We will show you
an example of what

00:29:59.660 --> 00:30:01.600
happens when we
start introducing

00:30:01.600 --> 00:30:06.950
AI, when you start using AutoML
into a Qualtrics flight survey.

00:30:06.950 --> 00:30:09.260
So with that, this
is video, and let

00:30:09.260 --> 00:30:11.007
me just go ahead and play it.

00:30:18.799 --> 00:30:19.760
Oh, hold on.

00:30:19.760 --> 00:30:20.260
Sorry.

00:30:27.750 --> 00:30:28.250
Hold on.

00:30:28.250 --> 00:30:29.220
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:30:29.220 --> 00:30:32.230
- You just got off your
flight, and we ask you a survey

00:30:32.230 --> 00:30:33.534
question, how was your flight?

00:30:33.534 --> 00:30:35.200
And let's say the
flight was uneventful,

00:30:35.200 --> 00:30:38.410
you have nothing special to
talk about-- you just say,

00:30:38.410 --> 00:30:40.330
it was OK.

00:30:40.330 --> 00:30:42.920
And in this example,
what will happen

00:30:42.920 --> 00:30:45.190
is, we know that you do not
have any specific intent,

00:30:45.190 --> 00:30:46.606
you do not have
any specific topic

00:30:46.606 --> 00:30:48.870
that you want to talk
about, so we will just

00:30:48.870 --> 00:30:51.360
end the survey right there.

00:30:51.360 --> 00:30:53.359
However, contrast that
with if I say something

00:30:53.359 --> 00:30:54.400
like, how was the flight?

00:30:54.400 --> 00:30:56.567
If I say-- I'm really
upset, and maybe I'm

00:30:56.567 --> 00:30:58.553
upset because they lost my bags.

00:30:58.553 --> 00:31:01.010
So I'll say, you lost my bags.

00:31:01.010 --> 00:31:04.160
Now in this case, we know
that you're not happy,

00:31:04.160 --> 00:31:08.410
and secondly, we also,
thanks to the AutoML APIs

00:31:08.410 --> 00:31:10.870
know what specifically
you're not happy about.

00:31:10.870 --> 00:31:15.330
So we will, A, acknowledge
that the person is not happy.

00:31:15.330 --> 00:31:17.670
This comes from
the ML APIs that we

00:31:17.670 --> 00:31:19.910
are able to understand the
sentiment in real time,

00:31:19.910 --> 00:31:21.285
but more importantly,
we are also

00:31:21.285 --> 00:31:23.700
able to understand what
is that specific topic

00:31:23.700 --> 00:31:26.310
that the customer has in mind.

00:31:26.310 --> 00:31:29.917
And we ask a more pointed,
a more contextual question

00:31:29.917 --> 00:31:31.000
around the specific topic.

00:31:31.000 --> 00:31:32.400
In this case, it's
about baggage.

00:31:32.400 --> 00:31:35.710
So we ask them a more specific
question around, all right,

00:31:35.710 --> 00:31:37.490
tell me more about
the baggage piece.

00:31:37.490 --> 00:31:40.060
So as you can see
here, by engaging

00:31:40.060 --> 00:31:43.300
with the customer on
a contextual level,

00:31:43.300 --> 00:31:46.660
it feels much more human like,
much more conversational,

00:31:46.660 --> 00:31:48.880
and that has resulted
in a higher engagement

00:31:48.880 --> 00:31:52.090
with customers and surveys, and
therefore a higher completion

00:31:52.090 --> 00:31:54.266
rate and a higher data quality.

