00:33:16.860 --> 00:33:20.760
So actually, that's
a good segue to--

00:33:20.760 --> 00:33:24.620
so this was the experience
that we could enable,

00:33:24.620 --> 00:33:26.620
where as you can see, it
was much more engaging.

00:33:26.620 --> 00:33:28.110
We were much more to the point.

00:33:28.110 --> 00:33:30.990
The questions that we asked
were much more relevant.

00:33:30.990 --> 00:33:32.760
And let's take a
look under the hood

00:33:32.760 --> 00:33:34.690
on how we made that happen.

00:33:34.690 --> 00:33:38.580
So I think Sudheera mentioned
around how the technology

00:33:38.580 --> 00:33:44.790
works, what we did was we took
a few of these responses for one

00:33:44.790 --> 00:33:47.490
of our customers-- you know,
when people give feedback after

00:33:47.490 --> 00:33:48.450
a flight--

00:33:48.450 --> 00:33:50.730
and we started labeling them.

00:33:50.730 --> 00:33:52.890
We labeled them with a
certain purpose in mind.

00:33:52.890 --> 00:33:58.330
So this company did have a team
working on flight cleanliness.

00:33:58.330 --> 00:34:02.400
So if somebody said that
the flights were messy

00:34:02.400 --> 00:34:04.470
or flights were not
clean, we label it

00:34:04.470 --> 00:34:06.360
as a cleanliness issue.

00:34:06.360 --> 00:34:08.340
If somebody said
that the flights--

00:34:08.340 --> 00:34:11.010
talked about that the
prices were too expensive,

00:34:11.010 --> 00:34:13.770
or the flight was
unaffordable and so on,

00:34:13.770 --> 00:34:15.750
we labeled that as
a pricing issue,

00:34:15.750 --> 00:34:19.350
because there is a team
that our customer has

00:34:19.350 --> 00:34:21.190
who works on pricing.

00:34:21.190 --> 00:34:24.900
So firstly, we label
a bunch of responses.

00:34:24.900 --> 00:34:29.980
After that, we train the model
on those varied responses.

00:34:29.980 --> 00:34:31.440
And it did a pretty good job.

00:34:31.440 --> 00:34:34.020
It had a pretty high
precision and recall.

00:34:34.020 --> 00:34:37.080
And we also tested
that by checking

00:34:37.080 --> 00:34:39.929
how good it is in
predicting what

00:34:39.929 --> 00:34:42.540
is the issue, what
is the category when

00:34:42.540 --> 00:34:44.500
somebody types a response.

00:34:44.500 --> 00:34:47.760
So in this case, this is
response that someone said,

00:34:47.760 --> 00:34:50.550
you're asking me to pay for even
the smallest things which were

00:34:50.550 --> 00:34:52.920
at one point free in a flight.

00:34:52.920 --> 00:34:54.960
Well, AutoML did
a pretty good job

00:34:54.960 --> 00:34:57.520
of saying, well, this
is a pricing issue.

00:34:57.520 --> 00:34:59.250
So in a real survey,
it would mean

00:34:59.250 --> 00:35:01.530
that this would get routed
to the pricing department

00:35:01.530 --> 00:35:03.540
in the flight company.

00:35:03.540 --> 00:35:10.380
And now, once this was
done, we used Qualtrics

00:35:10.380 --> 00:35:12.490
to help make a great survey.

00:35:12.490 --> 00:35:14.940
So for example, we have
this product Expert Review.

00:35:14.940 --> 00:35:19.454
What it does is it will
be able to predict,

00:35:19.454 --> 00:35:21.870
if you're creating a survey,
what's the right question you

00:35:21.870 --> 00:35:24.990
should be asking, and guides
you on how to ask that question.

00:35:24.990 --> 00:35:30.080
So it used the results of AutoML
to guide people, the survey

00:35:30.080 --> 00:35:32.730
creator, on what's the
right question to ask.

00:35:32.730 --> 00:35:35.850
We also have functionality
to have logic.

00:35:35.850 --> 00:35:38.950
So we said, all right, look, if
this customer starts talking--

00:35:38.950 --> 00:35:41.010
first of all, if
he's unhappy, then

00:35:41.010 --> 00:35:43.200
ask a certain set of questions.

00:35:43.200 --> 00:35:45.240
If he's happy, ask a
different set of questions.

00:35:45.240 --> 00:35:48.420
If they are talking about,
in this case, service,

00:35:48.420 --> 00:35:52.080
then ask questions around how
to you improve your service.

00:35:52.080 --> 00:35:54.570
So we made sure that
the logic in the survey

00:35:54.570 --> 00:35:56.220
was super contextual.

00:35:56.220 --> 00:35:58.350
And the net net
of it was the demo

00:35:58.350 --> 00:36:02.547
you just saw, where in
a flight-like survey,

00:36:02.547 --> 00:36:05.130
it's a very different experience
than what you would regularly

00:36:05.130 --> 00:36:05.730
experience.

00:36:05.730 --> 00:36:09.600
It's much more contextual,
a shorter survey,

00:36:09.600 --> 00:36:13.050
much more to the point.

00:36:13.050 --> 00:36:16.710
Let me wrap it up
with one last example,

00:36:16.710 --> 00:36:21.300
and this time I'm hoping
the video comes out right.

00:36:21.300 --> 00:36:23.100
So let's play this video.

00:36:23.100 --> 00:36:24.860
So this is an example--

00:36:24.860 --> 00:36:26.100
before I play the video--

00:36:26.100 --> 00:36:29.700
of an e-commerce site.

00:36:29.700 --> 00:36:31.830
So we also have a lot
of e-commerce companies.

00:36:31.830 --> 00:36:33.580
And you know what
happens with e-commerce?

00:36:33.580 --> 00:36:34.454
We've all been there.

00:36:34.454 --> 00:36:36.240
We all go to an e-commerce site.

00:36:36.240 --> 00:36:40.090
We all go and shop, but we
don't buy just right there.

00:36:40.090 --> 00:36:43.480
We browse, we take our time,
you may ask a significant other.

00:36:43.480 --> 00:36:45.870
Eventually we go back
and we decide on one

00:36:45.870 --> 00:36:47.550
particular product to buy.

00:36:47.550 --> 00:36:49.560
And what e-commerce
companies are trying to do

00:36:49.560 --> 00:36:52.260
is how can they make
that experience better

00:36:52.260 --> 00:36:53.480
for their customers?

00:36:53.480 --> 00:36:58.050
So how can they make sure
that the products they show,

00:36:58.050 --> 00:36:59.346
the selection, is right?

00:36:59.346 --> 00:37:01.470
How can they make sure that
the checkout experience

00:37:01.470 --> 00:37:03.060
is very seamless?

00:37:03.060 --> 00:37:05.580
So they want to get
feedback, and used

00:37:05.580 --> 00:37:06.850
us to get that feedback.

00:37:06.850 --> 00:37:09.300
But let's see how,
even there, we

00:37:09.300 --> 00:37:11.130
can ask the right
questions when you're

00:37:11.130 --> 00:37:12.610
trying to collect feedback.

00:37:12.610 --> 00:37:14.850
So for example, if somebody
is saying that, look,

00:37:14.850 --> 00:37:17.670
I wasn't able to find the
right selection of products,

00:37:17.670 --> 00:37:19.230
let's ask feedback about that.

00:37:19.230 --> 00:37:20.970
Let's not ask them
questions which

00:37:20.970 --> 00:37:22.770
are totally irrelevant
to what they're

00:37:22.770 --> 00:37:24.610
trying to get feedback on.

00:37:24.610 --> 00:37:25.645
So in this example--

00:37:30.170 --> 00:37:33.130
this is a survey that you
would have with Qualtrics,

00:37:33.130 --> 00:37:36.430
and here they ask, how was
your in-store experience?

00:37:36.430 --> 00:37:38.810
And it seems like
they're not happy.

00:37:38.810 --> 00:37:41.170
And we asked them why
were you not happy?

00:37:41.170 --> 00:37:43.810
And they said "The
service was great,

00:37:43.810 --> 00:37:46.670
but the checkout was
a poor experience."

00:37:46.670 --> 00:37:49.630
So now we use that-- thanks
to the AutoML topics,

00:37:49.630 --> 00:37:51.610
we ask a much more
pointed question.

00:37:51.610 --> 00:37:55.030
We ask them, all right, tell
me more about checkout--

00:37:55.030 --> 00:37:56.950
not service, not
product selection--

00:37:56.950 --> 00:37:59.260
we get straight to
the point, ask them

00:37:59.260 --> 00:38:01.060
specifically about that issue.

00:38:01.060 --> 00:38:04.610
And the survey is shot, it's
sweet, it's to the point.

00:38:04.610 --> 00:38:07.330
And net net, it's much
more engaging experience,

00:38:07.330 --> 00:38:13.060
and you get the answers which
can help you make decisions.

00:38:13.060 --> 00:38:15.780
So that's how we use
AutoML and Qualtrics

00:38:15.780 --> 00:38:17.940
to create engaging surveys.

