00:39:04.450 --> 00:39:06.070
AutoML Natural
Language has also been

00:39:06.070 --> 00:39:08.880
helping a lot of customers
in the news domain.

00:39:08.880 --> 00:39:10.630
Hearst newspapers, for instance.

00:39:10.630 --> 00:39:12.620
Let me tell you a
little example about how

00:39:12.620 --> 00:39:16.190
Hearst used AutoML
Natural Language to build

00:39:16.190 --> 00:39:18.970
their own custom classifier.

00:39:18.970 --> 00:39:21.880
News organizations
typically have

00:39:21.880 --> 00:39:25.240
a large repository
of ephemeral content,

00:39:25.240 --> 00:39:27.090
as well as evergreen content.

00:39:27.090 --> 00:39:29.590
By evergreen content,
I mean articles

00:39:29.590 --> 00:39:34.540
that can be resurfaced over
and over again to customers.

00:39:34.540 --> 00:39:36.730
These articles have
a longer shelf life,

00:39:36.730 --> 00:39:38.950
and they can be fed into
recommendation engines,

00:39:38.950 --> 00:39:41.500
they can be used to
personalize and increase

00:39:41.500 --> 00:39:44.410
the engagement of
these websites.

00:39:44.410 --> 00:39:47.110
So Hearst build a
custom classifier,

00:39:47.110 --> 00:39:51.820
and again, ephemeral content
versus evergreen content.

00:39:51.820 --> 00:39:56.840
If you were to tag news
articles with these two labels,

00:39:56.840 --> 00:40:00.250
any regular classifier
would not have these labels

00:40:00.250 --> 00:40:05.260
in the first place for you to go
ahead and build on top of this.

00:40:05.260 --> 00:40:10.850
This would require a lot of
manual model building process.

00:40:10.850 --> 00:40:15.790
So within a matter
of a couple of days,

00:40:15.790 --> 00:40:19.960
or hours in AutoML
NL's case, Hearst

00:40:19.960 --> 00:40:23.890
was able to load some
amount of training data,

00:40:23.890 --> 00:40:27.400
provide certain articles that
were manually picked and tagged

00:40:27.400 --> 00:40:29.780
by their editors as
ephemeral content

00:40:29.780 --> 00:40:33.170
versus editorial
evergreen content.

00:40:33.170 --> 00:40:37.490
And with that Custom
Classifier Model that they got,

00:40:37.490 --> 00:40:41.170
natural language typically
takes about four to five hours,

00:40:41.170 --> 00:40:44.780
based on our internal tests.

00:40:44.780 --> 00:40:48.490
So after four to five
hours of training,

00:40:48.490 --> 00:40:53.470
the Custom Classifier Model was
able to predict, with accuracy

00:40:53.470 --> 00:40:57.700
in the high 90s, whether
an article was ephemeral

00:40:57.700 --> 00:40:59.500
or whether it was evergreen.

00:40:59.500 --> 00:41:01.660
Now Hearst is able to
use that, and they're

00:41:01.660 --> 00:41:04.690
ready to deploy this
model into production

00:41:04.690 --> 00:41:10.510
to start to build recommendation
engines on top of this model.

00:41:10.510 --> 00:41:14.639
They're also trying to optimize
using AutoML NL the foundation

00:41:14.639 --> 00:41:16.930
that they've built with the
AutoML NL Custom Classifier

00:41:16.930 --> 00:41:21.410
Model to improve
their ad systems.

00:41:21.410 --> 00:41:23.650
So that's the power of
AutoML Natural Language.

00:41:23.650 --> 00:41:27.100
The simple process of
building a custom classifier

00:41:27.100 --> 00:41:32.590
can provide huge cost savings,
automate physical processes,

00:41:32.590 --> 00:41:37.560
and enable use cases that
weren't possible before.

00:41:37.560 --> 00:41:41.440
To quickly summarize,
if there was

00:41:41.440 --> 00:41:46.120
one key takeaway from this
session on both AutoML Natural

00:41:46.120 --> 00:41:50.920
Language as well as AutoML
Translate, high accuracy

00:41:50.920 --> 00:41:56.770
customization, and easy to
use machine learning models.

00:41:56.770 --> 00:42:00.730
These are the key
value propositions

00:42:00.730 --> 00:42:07.450
that we would like to summarize
the AutoML NL and the AutoML

